# Joint Final Design

## Problem Statement
Design a decision-based system that selects either Merge Sort or Quick Sort based on the requirements of the input data.

## Final Approach
After comparing both initial designs, we developed a combined decision-making approach.

The system considers the following factors:

1. Whether stable sorting is required.
2. Whether guaranteed O(n log n) performance is required.
3. Whether extra memory is available.
4. Whether in-place sorting is preferred.

## Final Decision Rules

- If stable sorting is required, choose Merge Sort.
- If guaranteed O(n log n) performance is required, choose Merge Sort.
- If memory usage should be minimized and in-place sorting is preferred, choose Quick Sort.
- Otherwise, choose Quick Sort.

## Comparison

| Feature | Merge Sort | Quick Sort |
|---|---|---|
| Best-case time | O(n log n) | O(n log n) |
| Average-case time | O(n log n) | O(n log n) |
| Worst-case time | O(n log n) | O(n²) |
| Stable | Yes | No |
| Extra space | O(n) | O(log n) average recursion space |
| In-place | No | Yes, in the usual implementation |

## Conclusion
The final design combines both students' ideas. It selects Merge Sort when stability or guaranteed performance is important and Quick Sort when in-place sorting and lower extra memory usage are preferred.
